WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.070
Have you ever felt that when
you are in your own house

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.390
cooking for yourself, that
you feel too comfortable, so

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:18.650
comfortable that you might
end up with a sad sandwich?

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:22.040
Well, this happens to me, too.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:28.440
But when my husband joins the
party it's certainly taco night.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.400
And of course, when you have
more help in the kitchen,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.390
the result is much better.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.350
Sadly, obviously, this
is not always the case

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.090
because working as a team can
be challenging too. Just imagine

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:48.410
a group of friends
trying to cook together.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.250
They are used to
different things.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.610
They are used to
use different tools,

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.010
and if they are not
all in the same page,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.220
it will be a disaster.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:02.590
Well, this often happens when
you put data engineer or data

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:07.390
professionals and infrastructure
engineers to work together.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.810
But if they speak
the same language,

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:15.040
they are said to build
successful products.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.120
In this talk, I'm
going to tell you,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.770
or discuss with you
some of the best

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.470
practices that you
could follow when

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.170
building Shiny applications.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.780
And some of the
challenges that you

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.310
may face when working with
interdisciplinary teams, when

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:37.020
you want to deploy Shiny
applications to the cloud.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.740
I am Ivonne Carrillo Domínguez,
the engineer manager at Bixal.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.470
At Bixal,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.370
we have several teams and
I've had the opportunity

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:50.480
to work in two of them, the data
science analytics, and cloud

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.750
computing teams, and these
are teams of 10 collaborate

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.090
together when building
data products.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.860
And the story that
I'm going to tell you

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:05.120
is about this team's very
first Shiny application.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.479
I've broken down this
story in three parts,

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:11.630
prologue conception,
development time, and going

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.960
to the cloud. In
each of those parts

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.690
I'm going to tell you some of
the challenges that we faced

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.630
and how we overcome them.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:21.530
And spoiler alert,
we should have

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.840
gone to the cloud
from the beginning.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.600
Let's start with the
product conception.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.790
The first question
that we ask is,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.190
how we are going to
collaborate, given

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.900
that we came from
the data analysis

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.940
that we were doing individually.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.490
Well, now we needed
to code together.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.340
You may know the answer, so
I'm just going to say it.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.410
We used Git.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.790
Some of the team members
were learning about Git.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:50.930
They have to push and pull code.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.890
Well, luckily,

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.850
we had this blogging
in RStudio that

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:01.200
can ease the learning curve, because
it can be tricky sometimes.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.450
And why use Git? Well,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.730
you want to be able
to share your code,

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.950
integrate everyone's
contribution,

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.710
and keep different
versions of your code.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:16.030
This is on your local
but also in the Git platform,

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.910
and you can use any Git
platform, like GitLab

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.520
or GitHub or Bitbucket.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.040
In our case, we asked the
infrastructure team,

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.550
which was a better tool because
what we wanted to do is just

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.610
focus on the development
and not worry too much

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.440
about the hosting.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.350
So we wanted some pipeline
and some automation

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:42.060
that we were where we
were able to push our code

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.110
and then it was going to
be deployed to the cloud.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.590
So they chose GitLab because
of the continuous integration

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:51.470
and continuous
development features.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:56.300
So in this way, they were able
to use the tool that they felt

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.920
more comfortable with while
we were using the tool

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.170
that we were comfortable with,

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:01.670
RStudio.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.770
Then we started using golem.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:13.510
Golem is a Shiny framework
to build production level,

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.600
robust Shiny applications.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.399
At the beginning,
I wasn't very sold

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:21.040
on using this tool
because it will

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.540
add a lot of R packages
and the scaffolding code

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.720
that I was very sure
that we were needed.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.820
But then I really like it.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.170
It it says you.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.270
A very basic web
application where

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.000
we can build on top of it.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.120
And one of the things
that it adds, for example,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:48.150
is these scripts that it
will get you from the step,

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.590
a step by step,
guide you step by step.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:54.360
So you can start
building your Shiny app.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.890
For example, it will help you
configure your application

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.940
name, your unit testing,
your favicon,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.940
and then it will take
you to the next step.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.860
So it is a very nice
tool and I highly

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.200
recommend using
golem or a framework

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.380
like this one, especially
if this is your first Shiny

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:14.320
application.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.970
Dependency management.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:22.510
This is a concept that if
you are not very used to it,

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:27.410
it can be hard to understand
it, but it is very useful.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:33.070
So we were facing the issue
that maybe my code was working

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.100
on my local, but it wasn't
working on another team

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.360
member, their environment.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.430
That's because they didn't have
the R package that was needed,

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.820
or maybe they have it

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.960
but it was an older version
that was wasn't supporting

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.230
the function that I had.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.650
So for that you
want to use a tool

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:53.230
like a dependency
management tool,

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.510
and there are several options.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:57.880
You might as well just
use an R script

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.580
that will call your
list of dependencies,

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.960
or there are more sophisticated
options, like packrat or renv.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.790
whatever the option you choose,

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:12.000
try to do it see
from the beginning

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.690
so you won't have to do
some reverse engineering,

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:20.100
remember all the tools that you
have installed, and then add

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.288
to the dependency
management tool.

00:06:22.288 --> 00:06:24.830
If you do it from the beginning,
you won't have that problem.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:31.030
Also it is OK to
explore an innovate

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.820
all the R packages
that are out there,

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:38.980
but for your application just
add the tools that you really

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.480
need for your
application to run.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.840
So when it is on the cloud,
you are going to use backups.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.770
Just the dependencies
that you need.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.040
So just keep it simple every

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:53.650
package that

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:57.663
you add issues, potential
blocks or issues

00:06:57.663 --> 00:06:58.580
that are more mundane.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.550
And so it's a good
idea to keep it simple.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.640
At this point, we were ready
for the development time.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.190
So everything is already set up.

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.355
So we were ready to
build our product.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.940
So how we were going to
distribute the workload.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:22.180
Well, golem helped you to
create these Shiny modules.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.560
You can think of them as
mini Shiny applications

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.890
that you are going to
call from the main script.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.980
In our case, we had
five dashboards.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.790
So we created one Shiny
module per dashboard

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.720
and we distributed them
among the team members.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.850
The way that you are
going to separate the work

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:45.105
is going to depend on your
use case, your application,

00:07:45.105 --> 00:07:45.980
or your team members.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:50.630
Integrating the back end.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:53.470
So this is how we did it.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:08:00.170
We collected the data and
created some R data frames.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:04.370
Then we loaded them into
the SQLite database,

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.980
which is a file.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:10.370
And then set up the
database connection.

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:14.060
We create all the queries that
we needed and then connect

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.500
them.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.910
Connect those results
to our dashboard chart.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.070
But that wasn't real data.

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:22.750
It was just like sample data.

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.180
So we needed to really
be collecting data

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:26.110
on a daily basis.

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:28.480
We needed to build
some data pipelines

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.340
and then load in that data

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:36.140
into an Amazon Redshift and
then do it all over again.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:38.020
So what is the
recommendation here?

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:42.530
Well, connect to your
database from the beginning,

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:46.220
even if that database does not
contain all the production

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:47.040
code.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.660
It can be simple data.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.420
But it is very important that
you start building your product

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.550
thinking that it's going
to be in the cloud

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:59.640
and then it's going
to be in production.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.290
Keep it secure.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.600
So at the beginning, we were.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:04.830
This is very bad.

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:08.430
I know, but we were hard
coding passwords, AWS keys

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.150
and other sensitive information.

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:11.730
So what happened?

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:16.300
Every time that we were
pushing database credentials,

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:20.710
we needed to go to that database
that was already compromised

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.530
and change the credentials.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:28.000
But what if we had pushed some
sensitive information that we

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.590
cannot really change?

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:34.900
Well, you need to go to Git
and remove that from your code,

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:37.450
but Git one of the
main features is

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:41.080
that you are going to keep
several versions of your code.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.020
So even let's say that we are
talking about the password,

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:49.090
even if the latest version
does not contain that password,

00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:51.130
you can just go back
to the previous commit

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.030
and the password is
going to be there.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:56.830
So you have to go all the
way back through the Git history

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:00.550
where that password is present
and remove all those commits

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.350
from the Git history.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:05.900
And by doing that, you are going
to remove some production code.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:07.180
So it's going to be a mess.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:08.390
So how to solve that?

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:11.330
Well, use environment variables.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:14.240
You can use these the dot
Renviron file that's

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.300
going to be kept in
your local that's

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:20.420
going to have these
key value pairs

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:26.050
and you are going to call the
keys in your code like so.

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:28.870
And not only is
going to be secure,

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.220
but it's going to be ready
for the cloud as well.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.060
Because what if you have a
database per environment?

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.390
Those credentials are
going to be different.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:39.040
It doesn't matter because
your code is already prepared.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.250
So only using the keys.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.080
The values are different
in every environment.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:46.870
So the only thing that you need
to tell your infrastructure

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:51.190
engineers are what are the
variables, the environment

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:53.710
variables that you need
through your application run,

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:58.220
and they are going to set up
with something similar to that.

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.440
Now going to the cloud.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.080
This is a moment of
truth, because what

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:06.020
you want at this point
is just push your code

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:07.920
and hope it runs.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.610
You want to build,
release and run.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:14.120
Well, this is rare that
happens the first time.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:17.870
And for us, it didn't happen
like the first 10 times.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.040
So how do we solve that?

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:23.420
What we did is that every
time, we were doing a commit,

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.070
we were validating that commit.

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:27.780
GitLab has

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.840
these nice pipelines
where you can add some

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.380
steps to validate the code
that it is being pushed.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:38.220
So, for example, what you
can do is the first step,

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:42.010
install the dependencies,
package your application.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:46.530
And I think this was the selling
point for me about golem

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:49.530
because I wasn't really
sure about using it.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.650
And, and when I saw
that goelm needs

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:57.030
to package your application and
then run it, it was weird to me

00:11:57.030 --> 00:12:00.390
because I was thinking like a
package like dplyr or

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:04.830
ggplot that those are
there for you to use it.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.990
And I didn't want my
application to be out there.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.500
But don't think you like that.

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.660
When you package your
application, what will happen

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.110
is that it will run
or you all your code.

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:19.620
So that means that if you
forgot to add a dependency,

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.090
it's going to fail.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:24.270
If you forgot to remove
some line of code

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.270
that they didn't have to
be, it's going to fail.

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:30.090
But if it can be
packaged, then it

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:34.770
means that every line of
code is a working line.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:39.150
So that's a very good step
for quality assurance.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:41.490
And then run
your unit tests, you

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:45.060
can validate the presence of
a title, a legend or a button,

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:50.310
just to know that your
dashboard is up and running.

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:53.550
Also have a development
cloud environment.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:57.130
This is an environment
that is meant to be broken.

00:12:57.130 --> 00:13:00.040
So it's for you to break and.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:04.510
If it is not broken,
then it means that you

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:05.870
can push it to production.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:10.550
And this is especially important
when you already went live.

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:13.270
And you can also have
a middle environment,

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:16.090
like a staging or
validation where

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:19.210
it's going to be an exact
replica of your production

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:20.030
environment.

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:22.990
And you can run some end
to end testing there just

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:28.240
to be sure that all the
functionality is there.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:32.590
So those are some of the best
practices that you can follow.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.210
And of course, there are more.

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:36.910
I will say that one
of the biggest ones

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:39.700
was the communication
between the infrastructure

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:43.140
engineers and the data team.

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:46.470
So I really recommend
you to get familiar

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.780
with all these concepts,
and I promise you

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:52.650
that communication
will get easier.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.170
Remember this group
of friends that

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.880
are in somebody else's kitchen?

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:01.390
It might feel weird
at the beginning,

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:04.230
but once they agree
on the terms that they

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.510
are going to be
collaborating, how

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:10.260
they are going to collaborate,
the work gets easier

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:14.400
and they can start working
in harmony and having fun

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:18.700
and most importantly,
have great results.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:20.520
Thank you.

